Step 1: Create the DynamoDB Table

We'll create it using Terraform to stay consistent with Infrastructure as Code.

Add the following to your main.tf:

####################################
# DynamoDB Table
####################################

resource "aws_dynamodb_table" "cart" {
  name         = "shopping-cart"
  billing_mode = "PAY_PER_REQUEST"

  hash_key = "cartId"

  attribute {
    name = "cartId"
    type = "S"
  }

  tags = {
    Name = "shopping-cart"
  }
}
Step 2: Deploy with Terraform

Run:

terraform plan

You should see:

Plan: 1 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy

Then:

terraform apply

Type:

yes
Step 3: Verify the Table

Run:

aws dynamodb list-tables --region ap-southeast-2

Expected:

shopping-cart

Or check in the AWS Console under DynamoDB → Tables.

Step 4: Install the AWS SDK

In your backend project:

cd Ecommerce-Devops-Project/Ecommerce-app/backend

Install the AWS SDK v3 packages:

npm install @aws-sdk/client-dynamodb @aws-sdk/lib-dynamodb
Step 5: Create a DynamoDB Helper

Create a new file:

backend/dynamodb.js

We'll write the complete code in the next step to:

Connect to DynamoDB
Add items to the cart
Retrieve the cart
Remove items from the cart
Why We're Doing It This Way

In many tutorials, DynamoDB access is written directly in server.js.

In a real project, we separate responsibilities:

backend/
│
├── server.js        ← API routes
├── db.js            ← PostgreSQL connection
├── dynamodb.js      ← DynamoDB connection
├── routes/
├── controllers/
├── services/

This modular structure is what you'll see in production codebases.

What We'll Build Next

After creating the DynamoDB table, we'll implement these APIs:

Method	Endpoint	Purpose
POST	/cart	Add a product to the cart
GET	/cart/:cartId	View cart contents
DELETE	/cart/:cartId/:productId	Remove an item from the cart

We'll also update the React frontend so that clicking "Add to Cart" stores the selected product in DynamoDB and displays it on the Cart page.

